import { ArgTypes, Canvas, Meta } from '@storybook/addon-docs/blocks';
import * as Stories from './ApplicationShell.stories';

<Meta
  of={Stories}
  summary="Provides responsive application chrome, section navigation and a fixed mobile action."
/>

# ApplicationShell

`ApplicationShell` provides the shared frame around an application's page content: a top bar, section navigation, a skip link and the main landmark. It accepts application-owned values and slot content without depending on an account or product domain.

<Canvas
  layout="fullscreen"
  of={Stories.Default}
  story={{ height: '844px', inline: false }}
/>

## Supply navigation values

Pass application values through `items` and map each value to the supported navigation fields with `getItem`. Every item needs a stable `id`, destination `href` and visible `label`; `icon` may use a curated Breeze `IconName`. Set `currentItem` to the ID of the current destination. The matching link receives `aria-current="page"`; in the top navigation it also carries the animated navigation marker, and in the bottom navigation its icon and label take the brand colour.

For example, an application can adapt its own route model while keeping the shell generic:

```tsx
<ApplicationShell
  account={<AccountMenu />}
  action={{ icon: 'add', label: 'Create item', onAction: createItem }}
  brand={<Brand />}
  context={<WorkspaceSwitcher />}
  currentItem={currentRoute.key}
  getItem={(route) => ({
    href: route.href,
    id: route.key,
    label: route.title,
  })}
  items={routes}
  notifications={<NotificationButton />}
>
  {pageContent}
</ApplicationShell>
```

The anchors keep native link behavior. When a same-document router is configured on `BreezeProvider`, section navigation sends `transitionTypes: ['nav']` to that router.

## Provide the application slots

`brand`, `context`, `notifications` and `account` accept `ReactNode`. Their content and semantics belong to the application: label interactive controls, keep keyboard behavior usable and provide accessible names for icon-only controls. `children` is rendered inside the shell's main landmark, stacked with a 16px gap. From Breeze's private `md` breakpoint (901px), a divider separates `brand` from `context`.

`action` is required. It renders as a circular, icon-only `IconButton`: `icon` selects curated Breeze artwork, `label` is its accessible name, and `onAction` performs the application-owned operation. Keyboard focus reaches it between the two halves of the bottom navigation.

## Responsive behavior and motion

From Breeze's private `md` breakpoint (901px), navigation is horizontal beneath the top bar and scrolls horizontally when its links do not fit; a focused link scrolls into view. Below that breakpoint, the navigation is a 72px bar fixed to the bottom edge, and the required action is raised over the bar's top edge in a fixed centre slot between the two halves of the items. The centre slot keeps its position as the current page changes. The main content includes clearance for the bar, the raised action and the device safe area.

<Canvas
  layout="fullscreen"
  of={Stories.OverflowingNavigation}
  story={{ height: '844px', inline: false }}
/>

The shell renders one skip link before its chrome and gives its main landmark a generated unique ID and `tabIndex={-1}`. Its top bar, top navigation, bottom navigation and current marker participate in view transitions through Breeze's built-in singleton names. Mount one visible shell at a time so those names stay unique in the document.

## API

<ArgTypes of={Stories.Default} />

